CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILD VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Article 2 Women and child victims of violence receive rights, including: a. the right to be respected for their dignity as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and according to needs;
h. the right of Victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
i. the right to Assistance;

j. the right to security; and
k. the right to restitution.

Article 3 Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 4, also receive special rights, including:
a. the right to survival, growth and development;

b. the right to basic population services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER III

PROTECTION OF WOMEN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Part One

General Article 4 The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

Article 5 Every person who hears, sees or knows of the occurrence of Violence as referred to in Article 4 shall make efforts to:
a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and
d. assist in the complaint process and submission of applications for protection orders.

Article 16

(1) The Regional Government shall prevent acts of violence against women by:
a. socializing laws and regulations;
b. conducting empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling;
c. making efforts to increase the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
d. making efforts to build community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence in an integrated community-based manner;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, religious institutions/organizations and various non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent violence against women and children;
f. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence;
g. raising community awareness that respects women and protects children;
h. establishing UPTD PPA in every Sub-district, and Women and Children Protection Service Posts at Village and Sub-district levels; and
i. establishing a women and children protection task force.

(2) In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions/organizations;
h. community social institutions;
i. business sector; and
j. other institutions.

(3) Prevention of acts of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 8

(1) The Regional Government shall implement integrated services for victims of acts of violence, through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. Counseling;
d. psychological services;
e. spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation services;
g. legal assistance and support services;
h. safe house services; and
i. repatriation and social reintegration.

(2) In the event that the Regional Government does not yet have counseling personnel as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, it may request assistance from social workers, support volunteers, and/or spiritual counselors.

(3) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and Safe Houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for the recovery of victims, including perpetrators.

(4) The provision of services to victims of acts of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with other social institutions.

(5) Other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner.

(6) Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraphs (1) through (5) shall be implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures.

(7) Provisions regarding standard operating procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 10

(1) Health services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph - (1) letter b, shall be implemented using health facilities owned by the Regional Government or other referral hospitals by providing medical treatment and health recovery services for victims.
(2) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided by health personnel to victims in accordance with professional standards, standard operating procedures and the medical needs of victims.
(3) Health services as referred to in paragraph (1), through efforts to:
a. take anamnesis of victims;
b. examine victims;
c. provide first aid;
d. restore health both physical and psychological, make efforts to prevent disease and other health disorders;
e. counseling;
f. refer to more adequate health service facilities if necessary may refer to other parties with a multidisciplinary approach, including health services, medicolegal, psychosocial support and legal assistance; and/or
g. provide information to the police.

(4) In addition to the efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) for certain cases, health personnel may provide reproductive health services in accordance with medical needs.
(5) In providing services as referred to in paragraphs (3) and (4), health personnel must create medical records in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(6) For every medical action to be performed by health personnel there must be informed consent from the victim or their family in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(7) For investigation purposes, authorized health personnel must prepare a visum et repertum and/or visum et repertum psichiatricum, or prepare a medical certificate.
(8) Health personnel are entitled to legal protection.

Article 11

Legal assistance and protection services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter g include:
a. providing protection in a Safe House;
b. providing legal information to victims;
c. providing support for victims as witnesses from the investigation process to the verdict;
d. providing special legal protection for child victims of acts of violence may be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with laws and regulations.

Article 14

Stages of protection for child victims of acts of violence, include: a. prevention of acts of violence against children;
b. handling of child victims of acts of violence; and c. rehabilitation of child victims of acts of violence.

Paragraph 2

Prevention of Violence Against Children Article 15 (1) Prevention of acts of violence against children as referred to in Article 14 letter a shall be implemented by:
a. establishing a network in efforts to prevent violence against children;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns with the community, private sector and non-governmental organizations; and
c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the protection of child victims of violence.

2. Prevention of violence against children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 16 (1) Prevention of acts of violence against children, in addition to being implemented by the Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (2), also involves the parties:
a. parents, family and / or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions / organizations;
h. community social institutions:

i. child social welfare institutions;

j. business sector; and
k. other institutions engaged in the field of care for child protection.

(2) Provisions regarding the involvement of parties as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Paragraph 3

Handling of Child Victims of Violence Article 17 Handling of child victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 14 letter b shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Paragraph 4 Rehabilitation of Child Victims of Violence Article 18 (1) Rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 14 letter c shall be carried out in the form of:
a. legal aid services;
b. health rehabilitation services;
c. social rehabilitation services;
d. repatriation and social reintegration services; and / or
e. restitution services for child victims of violence.

2. In rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency / city governments;
d. village / sub-district government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. community; and / or
h. family.

Part Two

Special Protection for Children Article 19 (1) Special protection for children shall be given to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and / or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/ AIDS;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale, and / or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and / or psychological violence;

j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities:
m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who become victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

.J (2) The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection for children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 21

1. P2TP2A as referred to in Article 20 shall have the duties to:
a. provide services;
b. provide protection to victims of violence, reporters and witnesses; and
c. conduct empowerment of victims of violence.

2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a include:
a. receiving complaints / reports of violence against women and / or children, consultation, and counseling;
b. receiving and sending case referrals from other Regency / City Regional Governments or institutions established by other Regency / City Regional Governments;
c. providing legal accompaniment / assistance;
d. health;
e. social rehabilitation;
f. legal aid services; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration.

Part One

Cooperation Article 22 In handling child victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 18, the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency / city governments;
d. village / sub-district government;
e. non-governmental organizations;
f. private parties;
g. community; and / or
h. family.

Article 23 (1) In certain cases the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. other Regional Governments;
b. police;
c. advocates;
d. health workers;
e. psychologists;
f. social workers;
g. volunteer assistants;
h. spiritual counselors;
i. other law enforcers, to assist Victims in the court trial process;

j. National Commission on Violence Against Women;
k. Indonesian Child Protection Commission or Child Protection Institution; and/or
l. certain parties desired for the benefit of victims.

2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. socialization;
c. provision of legal aid;
d. health services;
e. psychological / psychologist services;
f. victim assistance;

g. counseling;
h. social rehabilitation;
i. spiritual guidance; and / or
j. repatriation and social reintegration.

Part Two

Obligations and Responsibilities Article 24 Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to (1) women and children from acts of violence shall be the joint responsibility of the Regional Government and the Community.

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in (2) paragraph (1) include:
a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation activities in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutional protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to the financial capacity of the Region;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children victims of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and / or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of violence.

